  "title": "BSSMAP LE: Fix APDU IEI handling with length 32768 octets or more",
  "description": "BSSGP and GRPS-NS use a variable length field where bit 8 of the first\noctet is zero when there is a second length octet (3GPP TS 48.016\nSection 10.1.2, 3GPP TS 48.018 11.1). BSSMAP LE and BSSMAP do not\n(see 3GPP TS 49.031 10, 10.3, and 3GPP TS 48.008 3.2.2.0) and the\nlength indicator always indicates the number of octets that follow.\n\nThe APDU IEI in BSSMAP is a fixed two octets (see 10.3; compare the\nlabel \"Octets 2-3\" with how in BSSGP the length octet[s] are \"Octets 2,2a\".)\nThus the proper macro is not ELEM_MAND_TELV but ELEM_MAND_TLV_E; the\nformer is the variable length, whereas the latter is the fixed two octet\nlength in some IEIs. Any length less than 32768 (0x8000) placed in a\nfixed two octets will decode correctly with the variable length handling\nfunction (which is why this works on most cases), but it would be\nincorrect for the large large values.\n\nSee the example capture in #3711, which decodes fine either way, but not\nthat lengths that would fit in one octet are contained in two octets.\nThis is not illegal in BSSGP or GPRS-NS, see TS 48.016 10.1.2, but\nit is suggestive.\n\nAI-Assisted: no",
